vm-5 — Code generation: control / non-local exits / values (plan-2-bytecode-vm).

The remaining special operators — the ones with non-trivial control flow.

WHAT
- BLOCK / RETURN-FROM: lexical exit.  When the block is not captured by a closure,
  compile to a plain forward JUMP + a value slot (fast path).  When it may be
  invoked from an inner closure (escaping), fall back to a dynamic block frame
  (marker on the frame stack, RETURN-FROM unwinds to it) — mirrors the tree-walker
  frame model.  (Named-function implicit blocks are already lexical in the body,
  see [[bocl-defun-lexical-block]], so DEFUN needs nothing special.)
- TAGBODY / GO: intra-body jumps compile to JUMP for local tags; escaping GO uses a
  dynamic tag frame like BLOCK.
- CATCH / THROW: dynamic — push a catch frame (tag on the frame stack), THROW
  unwinds to the matching tag.  Reuse the C frame_catch machinery if practical.
- UNWIND-PROTECT: push a cleanup frame; any non-local exit through it runs the
  cleanup thunk.
- MULTIPLE-VALUE-CALL / -PROG1 / -BIND / VALUES / NTH-VALUE: use the VALUES opcode +
  the MultipleValues path already in the kernel.
- PROGV (dynamic bindings from a list), LOAD-TIME-VALUE (evaluate at load, store in
  the constant pool — ties to vm-8), EVAL-WHEN (compile the :execute body; the
  :compile-toplevel handling stays in COMPILE-FILE), FLET/LABELS (compile local
  functions as sub-templates + closures), MACROLET/SYMBOL-MACROLET (already expanded
  by vm-3).

ACCEPTANCE: compile+run RETURN-FROM (local + escaping), TAGBODY loops, CATCH/THROW,
UNWIND-PROTECT cleanup-on-throw, multiple values, PROGV; parity with the tree-walker
incl. the must-control / must-restart style cases.

DEPENDS ON: vm-4.

====================================================================
PROGRESS (v1.8.4, branch vm) — PART 1 of 2: lexical control done.

Lexical (non-escaping) control now compiles to byte-code JUMPs — no frames.  Since
compiled closures are vm-6, EVERY block/tag in a single compiled function is
non-escaping, so this is complete for the current VM:
- BLOCK / RETURN-FROM: forward JUMP to the block end; the exit value travels through
  a scratch local slot (allocated beyond the analyzer's n-locals) so the normal-fall
  and RETURN-FROM paths converge.  A RETURN-FROM taken from inside a sub-expression
  first CUTS the operand stack back to the block base (DROP, chunked to u8) — the VM
  is a stack machine, so the intervening partial values must be discarded.
- TAGBODY / GO: each tag is a label; GO cuts the stack to the tagbody base then JUMPs.
  TAGBODY returns NIL.  A codegen-time control-scope stack (in the compiler state)
  resolves RETURN-FROM/GO to the enclosing block/tag; escaping (via a closure) errors
  clearly (vm-6).
This lets the VM run real LOOPS: DOTIMES / DO / DOLIST (which macroexpand to
TAGBODY/GO/BLOCK) and early RETURN-FROM out of a loop all run and iterate without C
recursion.  Verified: tests/test_misc.c::test_vm_control (differential vs the
tree-walker, incl. a 1000-iteration countdown summing to 500500).  Full suite green.

REMAINING (PART 2 — dynamic NLX, next commit): CATCH/THROW, UNWIND-PROTECT, dynamic
(special) LET/LET*/PROGV binding save-restore, and MULTIPLE-VALUE-CALL / -PROG1
(hence MULTIPLE-VALUE-BIND/LIST, NTH-VALUE).  These need the setjmp/longjmp frame
machinery from eval.c (Frame + g_frame_stack; push_frame/pop_frame/find_catch_frame/
unwind_next_frame are static and must be exported) wired into the VM loop (new
opcode handlers BLOCK_OPEN/CATCH_OPEN/THROW/UNWIND_PROTECT/UNWIND_LEAVE/MV_*), plus
lifting the &optional-default/supplied-p/&key/&aux restriction with a prologue.  MV
codegen needs an mv-context threaded through %VM-GEN so single-valued tails set the
MultipleValues buffer.  (values) the FUNCTION already works via CALL-NAMED.
